{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","statement":"AIDS-related lymphoma is a disease in which malignant cells form in the lymph system of patients who have AIDS.","source_span":"AIDS-related lymphoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the lymph system of patients who have acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","statement":"AIDS is caused by HIV, which attacks and weakens the body's immune system.","source_span":"AIDS is caused by the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), which attacks and weakens the body's immune system."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","statement":"Signs of AIDS-related lymphoma include weight loss, fever, and night sweats.","source_span":"Signs of AIDS-related lymphoma include weight loss, fever, and night sweats."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","statement":"Lymphomas are divided into two general types: Hodgkin lymphoma and non-Hodgkin lymphoma.","source_span":"Lymphomas are divided into two general types: - Hodgkin lymphoma. - Non-Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","statement":"Non-Hodgkin lymphoma is more common than Hodgkin lymphoma in patients with AIDS.","source_span":"Both Hodgkin lymphoma and non-Hodgkin lymphoma may occur in patients with AIDS, but non-Hodgkin lymphoma is more common."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","statement":"When AIDS-related lymphoma occurs in the central nervous system, it is called AIDS-related primary CNS lymphoma.","source_span":"When AIDS-related lymphoma occurs in the central nervous system (CNS), it is called AIDS-related primary CNS lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","statement":"AIDS-related lymphomas are aggressive (fast-growing).","source_span":"AIDS-related lymphomas are aggressive."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","statement":"The two main types of AIDS-related non-Hodgkin lymphoma are diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (including B-cell immunoblastic lymphoma) and Burkitt or Burkitt-like lymphoma.","source_span":"There are two main types of AIDS-related non-Hodgkin lymphoma: - Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (including B-cell immunoblastic lymphoma). - Burkitt or Burkitt-like lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","statement":"Additional symptoms of AIDS-related lymphoma include painless swollen lymph nodes in the neck, chest, underarm, or groin and a feeling of fullness below the ribs.","source_span":"Painless, swollen lymph nodes in the neck, chest, underarm, or groin. - A feeling of fullness below the ribs."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","statement":"Diagnostic tests for AIDS-related lymphoma include physical exam and history, complete blood count, HIV test, lymph node biopsy, bone marrow aspiration and biopsy, and chest x-ray.","source_span":"Physical exam and history ... Complete blood count (CBC) ... HIV test ... Lymph node biopsy ... Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy ... Chest x-ray"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","statement":"AIDS-related lymphoma can occur outside lymph nodes in the bone marrow, liver, meninges, and gastrointestinal tract, and less often in the anus, heart, bile duct, gingiva, and muscles.","source_span":"Sometimes AIDS-related lymphoma occurs outside the lymph nodes in the bone marrow, liver, meninges (thin membranes that cover the brain) and gastrointestinal tract. Less often, it may occur in the anus, heart, bile duct, gingiva, and muscles."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","fact_refs":[2,8],"question":"What are the signs and symptoms of AIDS-related lymphoma?","answer":"Signs include weight loss for no known reason, fever, night sweats, painless swollen lymph nodes in the neck, chest, underarm, or groin, and a feeling of fullness below the ribs."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What causes AIDS, and how does it affect the immune system?","answer":"AIDS is caused by the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), which attacks and weakens the body's immune system so it can no longer fight infection and disease."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What are the two main types of AIDS-related non-Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"The two main types are diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (including B-cell immunoblastic lymphoma) and Burkitt or Burkitt-like lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is AIDS-related lymphoma called when it occurs in the central nervous system?","answer":"It is called AIDS-related primary CNS lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Which type of lymphoma, Hodgkin or non-Hodgkin, is more common in patients with AIDS?","answer":"Non-Hodgkin lymphoma is more common in patients with AIDS."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What tests are used to help diagnose AIDS-related lymphoma?","answer":"Diagnosis may involve a physical exam and history, complete blood count (CBC), HIV test, lymph node biopsy, bone marrow aspiration and biopsy, and chest x-ray."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_3","fact_refs":[10],"question":"Besides the lymph nodes, where else can AIDS-related lymphoma occur in the body?","answer":"It can occur outside the lymph nodes in the bone marrow, liver, meninges, and gastrointestinal tract, and less often in the anus, heart, bile duct, gingiva, and muscles."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","statement":"Mycosis fungoides and the Sézary syndrome are diseases in which lymphocytes become malignant and affect the skin.","source_span":"Mycosis fungoides and the Szary syndrome are diseases in which lymphocytes (a type of white blood cell) become malignant (cancerous) and affect the skin."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","statement":"Mycosis fungoides and the Sézary syndrome are the two most common types of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma.","source_span":"Mycosis fungoides and the Szary syndrome are the two most common types of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","statement":"In mycosis fungoides, T-cell lymphocytes become cancerous and affect only the skin; in Sézary syndrome, cancerous T-cell lymphocytes affect the skin and are also found in the blood.","source_span":"In mycosis fungoides, T-cell lymphocytes become cancerous and affect the skin. In the Szary syndrome, cancerous T-cell lymphocytes affect the skin and are in the blood."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","statement":"In Sézary syndrome, skin all over the body is reddened, itchy, peeling, and painful.","source_span":"Also, skin all over the body is reddened, itchy, peeling, and painful."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","statement":"A sign of mycosis fungoides is a red rash on the skin.","source_span":"A sign of mycosis fungoides is a red rash on the skin."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","statement":"Mycosis fungoides may go through four phases: premycotic, patch, plaque, and tumor.","source_span":"Mycosis fungoides may go through the following phases: - Premycotic phase ... - Patch phase ... - Plaque phase ... - Tumor phase"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","statement":"The premycotic phase is a scaly, red rash in areas of the body usually not exposed to the sun, causes no symptoms, and may last for months or years.","source_span":"Premycotic phase: A scaly, red rash in areas of the body that usually are not exposed to the sun. This rash does not cause symptoms and may last for months or years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","statement":"Diagnostic tests for mycosis fungoides and Sézary syndrome include physical exam and history, complete blood count with differential, peripheral blood smear, skin biopsy, immunophenotyping, T-cell receptor (TCR) gene rearrangement test, and flow cytometry.","source_span":"Physical exam and history ... Complete blood count with differential ... Peripheral blood smear ... Skin biopsy ... Immunophenotyping ... T-cell receptor (TCR) gene rearrangement test ... Flow cytometry"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for mycosis fungoides and Sézary syndrome depend on the stage of the cancer and the type of lesion (patches, plaques, or tumors).","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) and treatment options depend on the following: - The stage of the cancer. - The type of lesion (patches, plaques, or tumors)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","statement":"Mycosis fungoides and the Sézary syndrome are hard to cure, and treatment is usually palliative to relieve symptoms and improve quality of life.","source_span":"Mycosis fungoides and the Szary syndrome are hard to cure. Treatment is usually palliative, to relieve symptoms and improve the quality of life."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What are mycosis fungoides and Sézary syndrome, and what body system do they primarily affect?","answer":"They are diseases in which lymphocytes (a type of white blood cell) become malignant and affect the skin; they are the two most common types of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What distinguishes Sézary syndrome from mycosis fungoides?","answer":"In mycosis fungoides, cancerous T-cell lymphocytes affect only the skin, whereas in Sézary syndrome cancerous T-cells affect the skin and are also found in the blood."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are the four phases of mycosis fungoides?","answer":"The premycotic phase, patch phase, plaque phase, and tumor phase."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How long can the premycotic phase of mycosis fungoides last, and does it cause symptoms?","answer":"The premycotic phase, a scaly red rash on sun-unexposed skin, causes no symptoms and may last for months or years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What two factors determine prognosis and treatment options for mycosis fungoides and Sézary syndrome?","answer":"The stage of the cancer and the type of lesion (patches, plaques, or tumors)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_4","fact_refs":[8],"question":"Can mycosis fungoides and Sézary syndrome be cured, and what is the typical treatment goal?","answer":"No, they are hard to cure; treatment is usually palliative, aimed at relieving symptoms and improving quality of life."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","statement":"Primary CNS lymphoma is a disease in which malignant cells form in the lymph tissue of the brain and/or spinal cord.","source_span":"Primary central nervous system (CNS) lymphoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the lymph tissue of the brain and/or spinal cord."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","statement":"Primary CNS lymphoma can start in the brain, spinal cord, meninges, or the eye, where it is called ocular lymphoma.","source_span":"Primary CNS lymphoma can start in the brain, spinal cord, or meninges (the layers that form the outer covering of the brain). Because the eye is so close to the brain, primary CNS lymphoma can also start in the eye (called ocular lymphoma)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","statement":"Having a weakened immune system may increase the risk of developing primary CNS lymphoma.","source_span":"Having a weakened immune system may increase the risk of developing primary CNS lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","statement":"Primary CNS lymphoma may occur in patients with AIDS, other immune system disorders, or those who have had a kidney transplant.","source_span":"Primary CNS lymphoma may occur in patients who have acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) or other disorders of the immune system or who have had a kidney transplant."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","statement":"Diagnostic tests for primary CNS lymphoma include physical exam and history, neurological exam, slit-lamp eye exam, MRI, PET scan, lumbar puncture, stereotactic biopsy, and complete blood count with differential.","source_span":"Physical exam and history ... Neurological exam ... Slit-lamp eye exam ... MRI ... PET scan ... Lumbar puncture ... Stereotactic biopsy ... Complete blood count (CBC) with differential"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","statement":"A lumbar puncture, also called an LP or spinal tap, collects cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) from the spinal column for examination.","source_span":"Lumbar puncture: A procedure used to collect cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) from the spinal column ... This procedure is also called an LP or spinal tap."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","statement":"During MRI for primary CNS lymphoma, gadolinium is injected into a vein and collects around cancer cells so they show up brighter in the picture.","source_span":"A substance called gadolinium is injected into the patient through a vein. The gadolinium collects around the cancer cells so they show up brighter in the picture."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","statement":"Prognosis for primary CNS lymphoma depends on the patient's age and general health, the level of certain substances in the blood and CSF, where the tumor is located, and whether the patient has AIDS.","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) depends on the following: - The patient's age and general health. - The level of certain substances in the blood and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). - Where the tumor is in the central nervous system, eye, or both. - Whether the patient has AIDS."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is primary CNS lymphoma and where in the body can it start?","answer":"It is a disease in which malignant cells form in the lymph tissue of the brain and/or spinal cord; it can start in the brain, spinal cord, meninges, or the eye, where it is called ocular lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"What conditions increase the risk of developing primary CNS lymphoma?","answer":"A weakened immune system increases the risk, including in patients with AIDS, other immune system disorders, or those who have had a kidney transplant."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is a lumbar puncture and what is another name for it?","answer":"A lumbar puncture collects cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) from the spinal column by inserting a needle between two bones in the spine; it is also called an LP or spinal tap."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What role does gadolinium play in an MRI used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma?","answer":"Gadolinium is injected into a vein and collects around cancer cells, making them show up brighter in the MRI picture."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What four factors affect the prognosis of primary CNS lymphoma?","answer":"The patient's age and general health, the level of certain substances in the blood and CSF, where the tumor is located (CNS, eye, or both), and whether the patient has AIDS."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_5","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What tests examine the eyes, brain, and spinal cord to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma?","answer":"Tests include a physical exam and history, neurological exam, slit-lamp eye exam, MRI, PET scan, lumbar puncture, stereotactic biopsy, and complete blood count with differential."}
